\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Directional Hubble measurements from Migkas et~al.\cite{Migkas2020} fix an off-centre Sedov--Taylor\cite{Sedov1946,Taylor1950} / Lema\^itre--Tolman--Bondi (LTB)\cite{Lemaitre1933,Tolman1934,Bondi1947} shell geometry about a Centre of Expansion (CoE), then checked against the CMB\cite{Planck2018VII}. Both the Migkas sky-dependent Hubble rate and Planck isotropy of order \(10^{-5}\) are taken as facts; the goal is coexistence, not replacement. The observer (US) sits off-centre inside a thick ordinary-matter shell---the donut-universe---and we ask whether the gravitational last-scattering multipole can be cancelled by a kill function: an electromagnetic \(\Delta T/T\) multipole of kinematic--Doppler character, opposite in sign and matching in angular shape. Apparent late-time acceleration is an off-centre sampling illusion (no dark energy); a contracting geometry would share the average appearance but differ in angular signature.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Migkas et~al.\ report a directional cluster Hubble rate\cite{Migkas2020}; Planck finds CMB isotropy to \(\sim10^{-5}\)\cite{Planck2018VII}. We treat both as correct: Migkas anisotropy is free-fall about an off-centre CoE (Table~1; Figs.~2--4), and the CMB stress-tests that geometry. Observer at \(r=D\); anisotropy samples \(\beta(r)\), not \(a(t,\theta)\). Open problem: keep Migkas \(A(\ell)\) while CMB multipoles stay \(\sim10^{-5}\).

