\subsection{Einstein and Friedmann equations for the CoE+shell}
\begingroup
\setlength{\abovedisplayskip}{5pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{5pt}
\setlength{\abovedisplayshortskip}{3pt}
\setlength{\belowdisplayshortskip}{3pt}
The LTB metric is written about the CoE, not about us. The observer (US) sits at comoving distance \(D\) from that centre. Sky anisotropy is therefore an off-centre sampling of radial structure \(\beta(r)\), not a direction-dependent scale factor \(a(t,\theta)\). Einstein's equation is
\begin{equation}
G_{\mu\nu}=8\pi G\,T_{\mu\nu}.
\end{equation}
For concentric dust shells about the CoE the Lema\^itre--Tolman--Bondi line element is\cite{Lemaitre1933,Tolman1934,Bondi1947}
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{d}s^{2}=-\mathrm{d}t^{2}+\frac{(R')^{2}}{1+2E(r)}\,\mathrm{d}r^{2}+R(t,r)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\Omega^{2},
\end{equation}
with areal radius \(R(t,r)\), \(R'\equiv\partial_{r}R\), and \(\dot R\equiv\partial_{t}R\). Each shell obeys the Friedmann-like constraint
\begin{equation}
\dot{R}^{2}=2E(r)+\frac{2GM(r)}{R}.
\end{equation}
The interior mass uses a background density scale \(\nu\) and a radial mass multiplier \(\beta(r)\) (not a scale factor),
\begin{equation}
M(r)=\frac{4\pi}{3}\,R^{3}\,\nu\,\beta(r),
\end{equation}
so \(\beta=1\) recovers critical-density normalisation. In the CoE+shell geometry \(\beta(r)\) is small in the cavity, rises through the thick shell, and returns toward the exterior background beyond \(R_{\mathrm{out}}\).

Including radiation on the same radial scaffolding,
\begin{equation}
H_{a}^{2}\propto\frac{\nu_{m}\beta_{m}(r)}{a^{3}}+\frac{\nu_{r}\beta_{r}(r)}{a^{4}}-\frac{k(r)}{a^{2}},
\end{equation}
with \(H_{a}=\dot{a}/a\) and \(a(t,r)\) the local expansion factor of each shell. Early times are radiation-dominated (\(a\sim t^{1/2}\)); late times are dust-dominated (\(a\sim t^{2/3}\)). Curvature \(k(r)\) follows from \(E(r)\) and introduces no angular dependence into the metric coefficients.

