\subsection{Time evolution, donut universe, and CMB stress test}
In the donut-universe picture the CoE is fixed while thick shells expand outward; the sphere of events visible to us grows at \(c\). Recombination is a spherical surface of fixed physical radius around the CoE. The observer remains mid-shell between the inner and outer edges (S1 and S2). Critically, the Hubble-bubble volume about US satisfies \(V_{\mathrm{HB}} = 0\) until the observer passes \(D_{\mathrm{recomb}}\) (the CoE distance at which vision turns on). After that crossing, the visible sphere grows at \(c\). Scale discussion may be framed in terms of the time-dependent volumes \(V_{\mathrm{HB}}(t)\), \(V_{\mathrm{S1}}(t)\), and \(V_{\mathrm{S2}}(t)\) and the corresponding volume fractions of the Hubble bubble relative to the shell annulus and the outer sphere. How long the post-recombination ``oven'' ran before an observer existed remains an open timing question; the letter does not fix that interval, only the geometric sequence of shells crossing a fixed recombination surface while \(R_{\mathrm{vis}}\) grows at \(c\).

